{"table":"nodes","ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/circuit.seed_packets","record":{"id":"circuit.seed_packets","kind":"claim","label":"Exact small packet and weighted tail estimates","status":"unverified","source_id":"circuit-r2-26-source-seed","locator":"05-seed-simulation.tex:42–270; lem:seed-packet-estimates, eq:seed-initial-split, eq:seed-exact-special-norm, lem:seed-high-directions","statement":"Fix small b>0 before r→0. The finite ordinary initialization (cost b/r, mixer arcsin r, inverse cost, mixer π/4) splits exactly into an ordinary component and a special component of norm r. Its high directions ℓ_r,w_r are centered unit vectors, asymptotically orthogonal, and escape every fixed number sector. For bounded number-smooth tails, separated Weyl centers have overlap O(r^M) for every fixed M, while bounded displacements preserve the weighted tail estimates.","assumption_ids":[],"evidence_ids":[],"citation_ids":["circuit-r2-26-cite-seed-packets"],"boundary":"The full displaced packets may have occupation of order r^(−2); only their tails have uniform weighted bounds. No uniform whole-packet occupation bound is imported from the finite-word degree limit."},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/circuit.seed_packets","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/2f85413164f06365ea0d825befee447650da71d54080d5dd65e78a708891c560","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"short","subgraph_ref":"sha256:cfbe950801fd334d8fd957243fa3b1f716ef609148c5f2ff97d44f9a6443d82c/papers/short.json/support/0.3.1/77f0cf29ab577b42d8caa5b2831ebfb1ca170d4b13c1ef6897cadc8e93c2a834","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]},{"stage":"expository","subgraph_ref":"sha256:479155de953858e60f1454491ccda2215d88a4c2780e686cdfb8422481c62d9c/papers/expository.json/support/0.3.1/9dd5536bc567fc55efaa864733bf89747cea0268a8a3865dec51ddd8e2a235dd","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/qaoa-seed","anchor":"qaoa-seed","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/qaoa-seed","anchor":"qaoa-seed","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n\n<a id=\"seed-removal\"></a>\n\n### 8.4 Removing the seed controls without changing their joint law\n\nSeed removal must simulate one Gaussian variable per vertex, reused\nat all seed gates. Replacing each occurrence with a fresh independent\nGaussian would change the circuit. The source proves a theorem for\nany already fixed finite seeded word and any energy tolerance, producing\none finite ordinary word. Its proof works in the fixed-word tree\nlimit; it does not take a degree limit simultaneously with its small\nparameter. [OpenAI (2026b), §5, “Removal of seed controls”][q-seed-statement].\n\n**A small component supplies a large response.**\nFix $b>0$ and $0<r<1$, and put\n$$\nR_*=e^{-i\\pi X/4},\\qquad \\delta_r=\\arcsin r,\\qquad\n\\gamma_r=b/r,\\qquad e=Z\\mathrm{vac},\\qquad\n\\ell_r=Y e^{-2i\\gamma_r Zq(e)}\\mathrm{vac}.\n$$\nApply, in chronological order, cost $\\gamma_r$, mixer $\\delta_r$,\ncost $-\\gamma_r$ and mixer $\\pi/4$. Just before the inverse cost the\ninsertion is $v_{2,r}=\\cos(2\\delta_r)e+\\sin(2\\delta_r)\\ell_r$. Define\n$$\nd_{0,r}=\\gamma_r(v_{2,r}-e)\n       =-2br\\,e+2b\\sqrt{1-r^2}\\,\\ell_r,\n\\qquad W_Z(f)=e^{iZq(f)}.\n$$\nExpanding the first mixer and using the Weyl identity gives the exact split\n$$\nu_r=a_{0,r}U_{O,r}+a_{1,r}U_{S,r},\\qquad\nU_{O,r}=R_*W_Z(d_{0,r}),\\quad\nU_{S,r}=R_*XW_Z(-2\\gamma_re-d_{0,r}),\n$$\nwhere\n$$\na_{0,r}=\\cos\\delta_r\\,e^{i\\vartheta_r},\\quad\n a_{1,r}=-ir e^{-i\\vartheta_r},\\quad\n\\vartheta_r=\\gamma_r^2\\operatorname{Im}\\langle v_{2,r},e\\rangle.\n$$\nBoth component operators are unitary. The special component\n$\\psi_{S,r}=a_{1,r}U_{S,r}\\mathrm{vac}$ therefore has norm exactly $r$.\nIts normalized contribution to the insertion is\n$$\nw_r=u_r^*Z\\psi_{S,r}/r,\\qquad \\|w_r\\|=1.\n\\tag{8.1}\n$$\nThis is the direction amplified in the echo below. The two components\nneed not be orthogonal. The ordinary component has a bounded displacement\n$d_{0,r}=2b\\ell_r+o(1)$, whereas the special component contains a displacement\nof order $1/r$. Subsequent unitaries preserve each component's norm.\nOnly the undisplaced packet tails have bounded occupation moments;\nnorm remainders are carried by unitarity without assigning such bounds.\n\nThe source's high-direction lemma gives that $\\ell_r,w_r$ are centred\nunit vectors, become mutually orthogonal, and escape every fixed\noccupation sector. Splitting a uniformly bounded family of occupation\nmoments at a fixed sector cutoff shows that its pairings with these\ndirections vanish: first let $r\\downarrow0$, then send the cutoff to\ninfinity. The special component must nevertheless be retained because\nthe later echo magnifies its insertion contribution by $1/r$.\n[OpenAI (2026b), §5.2, exact initialization and high directions][q-seed-packets].\n\nA packet has the form $W(d_r/r)\\eta_r$, where the scaled centre $d_r$\nis bounded and the tail $\\eta_r$ has uniformly bounded occupation\nmoments. Packets with centres separated by a fixed positive amount\nhave overlap smaller than every fixed power of $r$. A short probe\nalong a Pauli axis $P$ sees a finite rotation determined by\n$-2\\operatorname{Im}\\langle v_P,d_r\\rangle$, as well as a small\nWeyl displacement. The direction $v_P=u^*Pu\\,\\mathrm{vac}$ is the\nactual current insertion. Toggling a cost rotates both its root Pauli\naxis and this direction; they cannot be selected independently.\n[OpenAI (2026b), §5, packet estimates][q-seed-packets].\n\nFor the finite list of nonzero special centres $h_j\\in\\mathbb R^2$,\nthe source constructs the same rotation $e^{-i\\theta X}$ at every\ncentre, with *zero clocks*: the signed pulse durations on each of two\nperpendicular axes in the $ZY$ plane sum exactly to zero. Choose the\naxes so that $c_{j,a}=h_j\\cdot P_a\\ne0$, and the square pairs\n$(c_{j,1}^2,c_{j,2}^2)$ are distinct except for equal or opposite centres.\nBrackets have zero clock components and generate $iX$ coefficients\n$c_{j,1}c_{j,2}F(c_{j,1}^2,c_{j,2}^2)$. Polynomial interpolation makes\nall these coefficients one. Equal or opposite centres have the same\nproduct, so they cause no obstruction to this common rotation.\n\nThis does not give arbitrary independent rotations: opposite centres\nobey $U_{-h}=XU_hX$. Exact finite reachability of the required common\nrotation follows by taking finitely many reachable conjugates spanning\nthe generated Lie algebra; their product map contains an identity\nneighbourhood, and subdivision reaches the whole one-parameter path.\nClosure alone would not give exact cancellation. The Lie-bracket and\npolynomial method follows [Li and Khaneja (2005), §II, Examples 1–2,\npp. 2–3](https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0510012v1); the finite-family,\nequal/opposite-centre and exact-clock assertions are proved in the\nQAOA source itself.\nThe distinction is exact: zero net probe clocks and the common target\nrotation are assertions about the finite ensemble of limiting centres.\nA finite pulse list realizing those assertions is fixed first. Its action\non the actual finite-$r$ packets is approximate. The separate accuracy\nlemma then follows the actual insertion drift\nthrough every probe prefix. Its ordinary-component error is $o(r)$;\nthe error on the normalised special component is $o(1)$ and hence\nalso contributes $o(r)$ to the state. These bounds are uniform over\nthe bounded-tail families needed for insertions, and their constants\nrefer to the chosen finite pulse list.\n[OpenAI (2026b), §5, “Selective rotations with zero clocks” and\n“Accuracy of selective probes”][q-seed-selective].\n\nThe shared seed family is essential even for a single spin. Two gates\n$e^{-itSZ}$ applied to $|+\\rangle$, with the same standard Gaussian $S$,\ngive mean $X$ expectation $\\mathbb E\\cos(4tS)=e^{-8t^2}$.\nReplacing the second seed by an independent copy gives $e^{-4t^2}$.\nAgreement of marginal seed laws does not preserve a circuit's value.\n\nThe error scale needed below follows from the source's quantitative\nprobe estimate. For a fixed finite pulse list, separated packet centres\nand bounded designated tail moments, write $\\varepsilon_r=o(r)$ for\nthe discarded vacuum remainder. At each probe prefix $j$, with current\ninsertion $v_{P,r}^{(j)}$ and huge-cost direction $v_r$, the proof gives\n$$\n\\max_j|\\operatorname{Im}\\langle v_{P,r}^{(j)},v_r\\rangle|\n \\le C_M(r^2+\\varepsilon_r+r^M).\n$$\nChoose a fixed $M>1$. The right side is $o(r)$: within each ordinary\npacket the relevant Hermitian expectation is real, distinct packet\ncentres have overlap $O(r^M)$, and terms containing only special\npackets have size $O(r^2)$. The norm remainder is carried by unitarity;\nit receives no unproved occupation bound. The exact zero clocks then\ncancel the first-order displacement on ordinary packets, leaving\n$o(r)$ error there. The prescribed action on a normalized special\npacket has $o(1)$ error, multiplied by its $O(r)$ amplitude.\nThese are the unequal accuracy requirements that the next echo uses.\n[OpenAI (2026b), §5, accuracy of selective probes][q-seed-selective].\n\n**The estimates must hold on the next input.**\nThe repeated echo uses more than the initial special norm in (8.1).\nLet $\\mathsf N$ count particles in the top child Fock space. Call a family\n$(\\eta_r)$ bounded smooth when\n$\\sup_r\\|(1+\\mathsf N)^k\\eta_r\\|<\\infty$ for every fixed $k$.\nAt each stage between simulated gates, the source propagates a linear\nordinary/special decomposition on every such family, with\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n u_r\\eta_r&=\\Psi_{O,r}(\\eta_r)+\\Psi_{S,r}(\\eta_r),\\\\\n \\|\\Psi_{S,r}(\\eta_r)\\|&=r\\|\\eta_r\\|,\\\\\n \\Psi_{O,r}(\\eta_r)&=\\zeta_r A_r\\eta_r+o(r).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $|\\zeta_r|=1$, and the low unitary $A_r$ and its adjoint preserve\nall these seminorms uniformly. A separate part of the induction transports\nthe special insertion:\n$$\n u_r^*Z\\psi_{S,r}/r=w_r+o(1),\n$$\nwith the negative sign between a selective flip and its undoing.\nThe norm remainders remain separate from the designated smooth tails;\nno occupation estimate is imposed on them.\n\nThis forward estimate gives the insertion approximation used at the next\ngate. For $P=Y,Z$, the vector\n$\\widehat v_{P,r}=A_r^*PA_r\\mathrm{vac}$ is itself a bounded smooth\nfamily. Since $A_r\\widehat v_{P,r}=PA_r\\mathrm{vac}$, unitarity gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|u_r^*Pu_r\\mathrm{vac}-\\widehat v_{P,r}\\|\n&=\\|Pu_r\\mathrm{vac}-u_r\\widehat v_{P,r}\\|\\\\\n&\\le\\|P(u_r-\\zeta_r A_r)\\mathrm{vac}\\|\\\\\n&\\quad+\\|(u_r-\\zeta_r A_r)\\widehat v_{P,r}\\|=O(r).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe second term explains why an estimate on the vacuum alone would not\nsuffice: its input changes with $r$. The scalar phase cancels in this\ncomparison. This $O(r)$ estimate supplies smooth approximants for actual\ninsertions; it is not divided by $r$. The singular echo instead uses the\nmore accurate difference derived next.\n[OpenAI (2026b), §5, packet invariant and insertion-approximation lemma][q-seed-echo].\n\n**The echo amplifies a controlled difference.**\nConsider a forward cost $t/r$, a selective flip of the special\ncomponent, the reverse cost $-t/r$, and a selective undoing of that\nflip. The packet invariant transports the special component so that\nits contribution to the insertion changes sign. For the actual\ninsertion directions before and after the first flip, it gives\n$$\nv'_r-v_r=-2r\\,w_r+o(r).\n$$\nThe Weyl product of the two costs is formed with these actual\ndirections before making any approximation. Their large centres\ncancel, leaving a bounded displacement\n$$\n\\frac tr(v'_r-v_r)=-2t\\,w_r+o(1).\n$$\nApproximating each direction by a low component with merely $O(r)$\nerror before this subtraction would leave an uncontrolled order-one\nerror. The remaining scalar Weyl phase is independent of the root\nspin and cancels in insertions; it need not itself converge. The\nsecond flip restores the transported special component so that the\nsame construction can be used again. Mixers receive analogous\nselective compensations. [OpenAI (2026b), §5, packet invariant,\ninsertion approximation and “One echo”][q-seed-echo].\n\nIn the limit, $\\ell_r$ and $w_r$ become two orthogonal external real\nGaussian modes at each root. The initialization uses $q(\\ell)$ and\nevery simulated seed gate uses the same $q(w)$: choosing\n$t=\\sigma/2$ implements $e^{-i\\sigma Zq(w)}$. Different vertices\nhave independent local copies. This is the required joint law of the\nseeded word. A finite induction uses the Weyl identity\n$$\n\\langle W(f)\\Omega,W(g)\\Omega\\rangle\n =\\exp\\!\\left(-\\tfrac12\\|g-f\\|^2\n                  +i\\operatorname{Im}\\langle f,g\\rangle\\right)\n$$\nto match the relevant finite Gram matrices. Escaping high directions\nsupply precisely the vanishing pairings between the new external\nmodes and endogenous child directions.\n[OpenAI (2026b), §5, external-mode limit][q-seed-gram].\n\n<a id=\"seed-energy-pairing\"></a>\n\n**The energy pairing after seed removal.** Matching the seed law and its\nfinite Gram matrices is not yet enough. For a centred low insertion\n$h=\\sum_j s_j\\otimes W(f_j)\\Omega$, the one-particle coefficient of\n$W(f_j)\\Omega$ is $i e^{-\\|f_j\\|^2/2}f_j$. Hence\n$$\n\\langle h,\\mathsf Lh\\rangle\n =-i\\sum_j\\langle s_j,+\\rangle e^{-\\|f_j\\|^2/2}\n                      \\langle f_j,h\\rangle.\n$$\nIn the seeded representation, $\\mathsf L$ places the root-seed factor\nin its vacuum and creates $h$ only in the endogenous child space.\nExternal components therefore contribute zero to the last pairing.\nThe high-direction estimate yields the same zeros in the actual\nlimit. The actual insertion differs from its low insertion by $O(r)$,\nso energy continuity completes the comparison. Matching Gram\nmatrices without this one-particle calculation would not identify\n$\\mathsf L$ or its energy. [OpenAI (2026b), §5, “Finite Gram\ninduction,” including the energy calculation][q-seed-gram].\n\n**Why the selective rotations are available at every required stage.**\nThe limiting projection of each special centre must be nonzero. The\nsource imposes finitely many visibility conditions on the angles of\nthe formal seeded word. At each relevant standard stage they have\nform\n$$\n-2\\operatorname{Im}\\langle v_Z^{\\rm form},\n        -2bz\\,e+yD^{\\rm form}\\rangle\\ne0,\n\\qquad z,y\\in\\{\\pm1\\},\n$$\nwhere $e=Z\\mathrm{vac}$ and $D^{\\rm form}$ accumulates echoes whose\nreverse costs have already occurred. These functions are real\nanalytic by the finite Weyl formulas. Setting all target angles to\nzero, while retaining initialization, gives\n$4bz e^{-8b^2}\\ne0$, so none is identically zero. An arbitrarily small\nangle perturbation avoids their finite union of zero sets and the\nzero echo-time hyperplanes. The huge-cost stages need a separate\ntransverse-projection estimate; their limiting projection pairs then\nalso have a nonzero coordinate. Finite pulse lists can now be chosen\nsuccessively from limits already established at earlier stages.\nNo list is chosen from a limit that assumes its own successful\nimplementation. [OpenAI (2026b), §5, huge-stage transverse projection,\nvisibility and construction order][q-seed-visibility].\n\nThe choices are therefore nested: make $b$ small to control\ninitialization; perturb the fixed target angles to ensure visibility;\nchoose all finite probe lists; finally choose $r$ small. A value of\n$r$ fixes every large angle and every finite product. The theorem\nallows those angles to be large or negative. It makes no claim of\nuniform bounds as the target word length grows or as the tolerance\ntends to zero. [OpenAI (2026b), §5, proof of seed removal][q-seed-visibility].\n\n","span":{"start_byte":142816,"end_byte":156159,"start_line":3144,"end_line":3414},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=qaoa-seed","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=qaoa-seed"}],"lenses":{"prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=circuit.seed_packets&assumptions=true","consumers":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=circuit.seed_packets&direction=consumers&assumptions=true","direct_passages":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=circuit.seed_packets&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=circuit.seed_packets&relation=prerequisites"},"relations":[{"family":"arguments","id":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/edges/%5B%22circuit.seed_packets%22%2C%22circui